## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of bit manipulation algorithms and performance optimization. The SWAR approach demonstrates understanding of parallel processing at the bit level and reveals trade-offs between naive implementations and optimized solutions.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) for bit-level parallelism
- Strategic masking and shifting for parallel accumulation
- Branchless algorithms minimizing pipeline stalls
- Cross-platform compiler compatibility and correctness

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x) { 
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Parity computation over 128 bits tests the ability to design constant-time reductions using only bitwise operations and shifts, without loops or branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise XOR folding across 128 bits
- Progressive reduction by shifting (64,32,16,8,4,2,1)
- Final extraction with a single bit AND

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128_t;
unsigned parity128(uint128_t x) {
  uint64_t v = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  v ^= v >> 32;
  v ^= v >> 16;
  v ^= v >> 8;
  v ^= v >> 4;
  v ^= v >> 2;
  v ^= v >> 1;
  return v & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to 256 bits while preserving the same approach?
- Can you prove the operation is constant time regardless of input bit distribution?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 64-bit field layouts and perform cross-field moves without branches, a common pattern in graphics and encoding pipelines.

## Key Concepts
- Bit masking for 16-bit lanes
- Cross-lane permutation with shifts
- No loops/branches; constant-time operations

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t swapR_B(uint64_t v) {
  uint64_t r = v & 0x000000000000FFFFULL;
  uint64_t g = v & 0x00000000FFFF0000ULL;
  uint64_t b = v & 0x0000FFFF00000000ULL;
  uint64_t a = v & 0xFFFF000000000000ULL;
  return (r << 32) | g | (b >> 32) | a;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to swap G and A as well? 
- Analyze performance implications on 64-bit architectures with compiler optimizations.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about cross-boundary extraction and 128-bit layouts; practical in serialization, bitboard ops, or crypto.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-lane bit extraction
- Masking and shifts
- Boundary checks

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t extractWord128(uint128 x, unsigned pos){
  switch(pos){
    case 0: return (uint32_t)(x.lo & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
    case 1: return (uint32_t)((x.lo >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
    case 2: return (uint32_t)(x.hi & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
    default: return (uint32_t)((x.hi >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
  }
}
```

