## Why This Is Asked
Tests precise understanding of bit-rotation, modulo-64 behavior, and edge cases without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Rotation via (x << s) | (x >> (64 - s)) with s = n & 63
- Safe when s = 0 using masking (64 - s) & 63
- No loops or conditionals; only shifts and ORs

## Code Example
```javascript
// JavaScript BigInt version for 64-bit rotation
function rotl64(x, n) {
  const s = BigInt(n & 63);
  const MASK = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFn;
  return ((x << s) | (x >> ((64n - s) & 63n))) & MASK;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 128-bit types?
- What compiler optimizations help ensure constants are folded efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests low-level bitboard thinking, edge masking, and 128-bit manipulation without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 8x16 bitboard mapping in 128 bits (bit i = row*16 + col).
- Horizontal moves use 1-bit shifts with edge masks (COL0/COL15).
- Vertical moves use 16-bit shifts with TOP/BOTTOM masks.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode close to C for clarity
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
uint128 expandMask128(uint128 m){
  uint128 left  = (m & ~COL0) >> 1;
  uint128 right = (m & ~COL15) << 1;
  uint128 up    = (m & ~TOP) << 16;
  uint128 down  = (m & ~BOTTOM) >> 16;
  return or128(or128(left, right), or128(up, down));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for an irregular 3x5 tile grid embedded in 128 bits?
- What are the performance implications of branching-free bitboard ops on modern CPUs?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates non-branching, low-level bit-twiddling mastery and tests the ability to work with 128-bit data using traditional C structures.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount algorithm
- Handling 128-bit values via hi/lo halves
- Non-branching reduction using masks and shifts

## Code Example
```c
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t x) {
    x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) + ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL);
    x = (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
    x = (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
    x = (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) + ((x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL);
    return (unsigned int)x;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
    return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```
